+The main manuscript reports three findings: a FLOPs-proportional proxy does not reproduce measured feasibility; weight sharing costs the largest capacity 0.9--2.8 \miou{} points relative to training it alone; and, once routes and static deployments are timed in the same harness on the two primary backends, routing under a hard budget beats rank escalation but not static deployment; in a counterfactual overhead-scaling analysis, the static advantage would vanish under mean-cost budgets only below about 1.3~ms (AGX Xavier) and 4.3~ms (Hailo-8) of overhead. This supplement contains the material needed to reproduce and audit these results. Operating cells are correlated evaluations, and the two devices share segmentation predictions. Two budget grids are used: the route-budget grid (3 runs $\times$ 2 backends $\times$ 5 splits $\times$ 4 budgets) and, for the comparison with static deployment, a static-own-cost grid per backend (3 runs $\times$ 5 splits $\times$ 8 budgets), part of which lies below the cheapest route.
 
