+\paragraph{Break-even interval} An image-level bootstrap (200 replicates, fixed seed) of the counterfactual mean-cost break-even overhead gives 1.27~ms [0.57, 1.50] on AGX Xavier and 4.26~ms [2.13, 5.15] on Hailo-8, against measured overheads of 1.9~ms and 44~ms; every replicate crossed zero. The interval covers image sampling only; the overhead grid is coarse, and seed and timing variability are not included.
+
 \section{Additional qualitative examples}
 
 All examples are held-out images of Run A, fixed by rules applied to the recorded per-image errors before visual inspection: per split, the image with the largest tiny-minus-large pixel error (Fig.~\ref{fig:s-gain}), the image nearest the median large-candidate error (Fig.~\ref{fig:s-median}), the image with the largest large-candidate error (Fig.~\ref{fig:s-hard}), and, among images with below-median tiny error, the one with the smallest tiny-minus-large difference (Fig.~\ref{fig:s-easy}). The routed output is policy D on AGX Xavier at the budget of the large route. Dashed boxes mark the window where tiny and large disagree most; insets enlarge it; orange in the error column marks disagreement with ground truth.
